WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
After DNA is copied, why do daughter cells not simply forget which genes were

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.640
silent? The first film showed that access and regulators shape output.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.860
Now follow one regulatory state through replication and division.

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.240
The sequence survives, but the state still needs maintenance.

5
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:26.463
Replication copies the DNA sequence.

6
00:00:27.763 --> 00:00:32.083
It does not automatically place every chemical mark on both new molecules.

7
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:39.303
Look at a methylated CpG site, where a C is followed by a G. Each daughter duplex

8
00:00:39.303 --> 00:00:41.223
first has one old marked strand.

9
00:00:42.603 --> 00:00:44.683
Its partner is a new unmarked strand.

10
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.213
The two strands now differ at each marked site.

11
00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:52.113
One carries inherited positional information.

12
00:00:53.413 --> 00:00:55.633
The other carries freshly copied sequence.

13
00:00:56.873 --> 00:01:02.153
A maintenance system must distinguish them before both daughter molecules can regain

14
00:01:02.153 --> 00:01:03.173
a similar pattern.

15
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:10.623
Now predict. Which strand can guide restoration of the methylation pattern: the old

16
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:13.343
marked strand or the new unmarked strand?

17
00:01:14.443 --> 00:01:18.163
Hold both possibilities beside the same CpG positions.

18
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.927
The old marked strand supplies the template.

19
00:01:28.347 --> 00:01:34.207
Guided to these half-marked sites, DNMT1 copies the old pattern onto the new strand.

20
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.687
It is the main maintenance enzyme here.

21
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:41.607
Other methyltransferases lay down new patterns.

22
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:48.810
In mammals, dense methylation of a promoter CpG island goes with a silent gene,

23
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.050
within promoter-specific limits.

24
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.690
A restored pattern can therefore help keep that promoter quiet in both daughters.

25
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:03.110
When the pattern is restored after replication, both daughters can inherit a similar

26
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:08.110
regulatory state. That does not guarantee identical expression.

27
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:13.730
It helps preserve the conditions under which the gene remains hard to read.

28
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.890
Similar does not mean perfectly fixed.

29
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:23.573
An epigenetic change is inherited across cell divisions without changing DNA

30
00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:29.873
sequence. Mitotic inheritance is well established through DNMT1 and more.

31
00:02:31.373 --> 00:02:36.333
X inactivation and imprinting can keep one gene copy silent through many divisions.

32
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:41.337
Inheritance through a whole animal's germline is a different claim.

33
00:02:42.597 --> 00:02:45.457
It is documented in plants and in C. elegans.

34
00:02:46.737 --> 00:02:52.197
In mammals, most DNA methylation is erased and rewritten in developing germ cells

35
00:02:52.197 --> 00:02:53.897
and in the early embryo.

36
00:02:55.297 --> 00:03:00.237
That is one reason transgenerational claims remain limited and contested.

37
00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:03.867
So chemical marks are not permanent ink.

38
00:03:05.587 --> 00:03:10.307
Maintenance machinery must restore a state after replication, and other regulators

39
00:03:10.307 --> 00:03:14.727
can oppose it. Cellular memory is an active process.

40
00:03:16.187 --> 00:03:18.507
Replication creates the need again each time.

41
00:03:19.727 --> 00:03:23.367
It is not evidence that every acquired state crosses generations.
